    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d63">

                <pb facs="0157" n="67" sameAs="frus:frus1920v02/pg67"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">837.73/6: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in Cuba</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Long</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-11-11T16:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">November 11,
                                1920—4 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>232. Your despatch 253, July 10th, in regard to the permit for a cable landing
                    granted the Western Union Telegraph Company by the Cuban Government.</p>
                <p>You will inquire carefully and make a full investigation relative to the cable
                    situation in question. If any kind of cable has been laid under the provisions
                    of the permit, or if there have been any steps taken, or if any are under
                    consideration, you will reply by telegram and will keep the Department informed
                    of any developments.</p>
                <p>The application for a cable landing at Miami for the Barbados-Brazil cable has
                    been withdrawn by the Western Union Company. It is believed that an attempt may
                    be made by the Western Union to make a cable connection with Cuba at some point
                    near Miami beyond the territorial waters of the United States, and to use the
                    existing cables from Cuba to Key West for the transmission of messages.<anchor
                        n="73" xml:id="d63-f1"/> You will make a careful investigation
                    immediately.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Colby</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
